## 5. REGRESSION — global cspace enlargement is regression-clean

Per acceptance (3) ("capability accounting change is global — prove
the rest of the system is unaffected"), the five required scenarios
were run at the 16384 config (1ST=7, 2ND=7 — the booting global cspace
enlargement). All PASS. This proves that enlarging the per-PD CSpace
does not regress the rest of the system; the only consumer that needs
more than 16384 is falkon itself.

  scenario               config        result   gate
  ---------------------  ------------  -------  -------------------------------
  sponge-boot            16384 / sel4  PASS     boot-probe: PASS
  sponge-terminal        16384 / sel4  PASS     terminal-probe: PASS
  sponge-alpha           16384 / sel4  PASS     alpha-probe: PASS
  sponge-desktop-disk    16384 / sel4  PASS     alpha-probe: PASS
  sponge-persist-disk    16384 / sel4  PASS     restored 1 root(s) from store

Per-scenario logs: docs/evidence/p4-regress/<scenario>.log
Summary:           docs/evidence/p4-regress/SUMMARY.txt

NOTE on sponge-boot harness: it lacks `ensure_plugin_loaded boot_dir/sel4`,
so a bare `RUN_OPT=--include image/disk` override drops the boot_dir
plugin and fails instantly ("boot_dir module missing"). The re-run used
the full `RUN_OPT='--include boot_dir/sel4 --include power_on/qemu
--include log/qemu --include image/disk'` and passed. This is a run-
script harness asymmetry (the newer P2/P4 scripts self-load plugins;
the P1 sponge-boot does not), NOT a regression.

After the regression set, the vendored tree was reverted to pristine
(2ND=7, 1ST=6) and `sponge-boot` re-run on the pristine config to
confirm baseline health: `boot-probe: PASS (22 bytes:
"sponge-boot-marker-v1.")`.

